<Window x:Class="D47.Panel.MainWindow"
        x:ClassModifier="internal"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:render="clr-namespace:D47.Render;assembly=D47.Render"
        Title="Directive 47"
        AutomationProperties.Name="Directive 47">

    <!--
        Deliberately no Height or Width. The controls decide how big the render
        is and the window opens around them, which is a number nobody can type
        here — it is worked out in MainWindow.xaml.cs, once, as the window is
        being created. What used to be here was 960x720, chosen before there was
        anything in the window to disagree with it.

        Deliberately no SizeToContent either, which is the WPF way to say the
        same thing and says too much of it. It fits the window to its content
        every time the content changes, so the size stops being the Commander's
        the moment a view is switched. It was tried anyway, on the theory that it
        could be turned off again once it had fitted the window once, and there
        is no event that fires between the two: the layout pass that applies the
        fit reports itself finished before the new size is on the window, so
        turning it off there froze the panel at the smallest size Windows will
        draw.

        Deliberately no Background. There is one render, it fills the window, and
        the background is a property of what that render draws — see
        D47.Render/Palette.cs. This window naming one of its own is how it came
        to disagree with the render in the first place.
    -->

    <!--
        Below what fits, it scrolls. The fitted size is where the window opens
        and the size at which everything is visible at once, not a floor the
        Commander is held to — so a window dragged smaller than its controls
        moves them rather than shrinking them.

        Vertically only, and that is the reflow rule rather than a shortcut.
        Content that runs out of width wraps and goes down the window; content
        that runs out of height has nowhere left to go, which is where scrolling
        starts. Horizontal scrolling would take the wrapping away — a child
        measured against infinite width never wraps at all — and put a second
        scrollbar in place of it.
    -->
    <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                  HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
        <render:CapabilityView x:Name="View" />
    </ScrollViewer>

</Window>
